## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to compare 128-bit values split across lanes and derive a scalar metric without 128-bit math.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit data split into hi/lo lanes
- XOR to highlight differences
- 64-bit popcount for bit counting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Reference approach in C-like pseudocode; interview would use C/C++
int hammingDistance128(uint64_t a_hi, uint64_t a_lo, uint64_t b_hi, uint64_t b_lo) {
  uint64_t dh = a_hi ^ b_hi;
  uint64_t dl = a_lo ^ b_lo;
  return __builtin_popcountll(dh) + __builtin_popcountll(dl);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to implement popcount without builtins?
- What if values are stored in different endianness?
- How would this extend to 256-bit values?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates non-branching, low-level bit-twiddling mastery and tests the ability to work with 128-bit data using traditional C structures.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount algorithm
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves
- Non-branching reduction using masks and shifts

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    x = (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL);
    return (unsigned int)x;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests advanced bit manipulation skills. A palindrome check requires full 128-bit reversal and a single branchless comparison. It assesses understanding of endianness, cross-lane bit operations, and how to compose a complex operation from a reusable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit bit reversal (SWAR-style steps)
- Branchless comparison via bitwise XOR
- Endian-agnostic bit ordering across two 64-bit lanes

## Code Example
```c
int isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x) {
    uint128 r = reverseBits128(x);
    uint128 d = x ^ r;
    return (d == 0) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests extending known 64-bit tricks to 128-bit values with branchless code.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit counting
- 64-bit popcount kernel
- Combining results across two words

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x -= (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint8_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  return (uint8_t)(popcount64(hi) + popcount64(lo));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIMD/vectorized paths?
- How would you test correctness across random 128-bit inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Branchless 128-bit comparison is common in crypto/hash pipelines and requires careful borrow logic across halves. It tests low-level reasoning without relying on 128-bit native ops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo.
- Borrow propagation across 64-bit halves.
- Branchless evaluation using comparisons on unsigned integers.

## Code Example
```c
uint8_t lessThan128(uint128 a, uint128 b){
  uint64_t loDiff = a.lo - b.lo;
  uint64_t borrowLo = (a.lo < b.lo);
  uint64_t hiDiff = a.hi - b.hi - borrowLo;
  uint64_t borrowHi = (a.hi < b.hi + borrowLo);
  return (uint8_t)borrowHi;
}
```

