- How to extend to 128-bit equality without extra branches?
- How would you adapt for signed 128-bit comparison?

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to extend bit manipulation techniques to multi-register data structures while maintaining branchless performance.

## Key Concepts
- Two's complement negation for 64-bit words
- Isolating least significant set bit with x & -x
- Branchless masking using arithmetic on boolean predicates

## Code Example
```javascript
void isolateLeastBit128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_lsb = lo & -lo;
  uint64_t hi_lsb = hi & -hi;
  uint64_t nonzero_lo = -(lo != 0);
  uint64_t zero_lo = -(lo == 0);
  *out_lo = lo_lsb & nonzero_lo;
  *out_hi = hi_lsb & zero_lo;
}
```

## Example
Input: hi=0x8000, lo=0x0C00 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0400
Input: hi=0x0000, lo=0x0000 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0000

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of per-byte bit-twiddling, branchless design, and parallel bit tricks that map well to performance-critical code paths in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- Byte-wide parity, parallel bit tricks, constant-time operations
- Masking to extract per-byte indicators
- No branches or loops

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t perByteParity64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t y = x;
  y ^= y >> 1;
  y ^= y >> 2;
  y ^= y >> 4;
  return y & 0x0101010101010101ULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 32-bit blocks? 
- Can you adapt to compute even parity per byte instead of odd parity?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply bit-dilation technique and Morton code basics, a common need in graphics or spatial indexing.

## Key Concepts

- Bit spreading (dilate) with masks
- Interleaving two bit streams into a 64-bit value
- No-loops approach with fixed masks

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t morton2D64(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  uint64_t dx = x;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  uint64_t dy = y;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  return dx | (dy << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 3D Morton code with x,y,z?
- What are performance considerations on 64-bit vs 32-bit platforms and compiler optimizations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level folding to compute parity with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- Parity calculation
- Bitwise shifts and XORs
- Constant-time operations

## Code Example
```javascript
uint8_t parity32(uint32_t x){
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return x & 1;
}
```

